g to work are changing. So they conducted a survey, and the results are shown below.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Subway** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| **Subway** | $0.5$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nThey found that $50%$ of those surveyed that currently drive to work are planning to switch to the subway next year, while $60%$ of the people surveyed that currently use the subway are planning to switch to their cars next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $50\\,000$ that travel to work by car or subway, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use their cars to drive to work? Round your answer to the nearest hundreds."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267237} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267263} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267778} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268142} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267237} :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268650}] :question/math-academy-id "q-135408" :question/problem "There are two groups of people In a small town: those who travel to work by car and those who travel by train. Every year a study is done in which people are asked about their preference between using trains versus cars. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Train** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Train** | $0.65$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $65%$ of people who currently travel by car will switch to train next year, while $25%$ of people who currently travel by train will switch to their car next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $90\\,000$ people that travel to work by train or car, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use the train to travel to work?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266530} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266597} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267042} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267205} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267313} :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268607}] :question/math-academy-id "q-135409" :question/problem "A factory produces two types of sugar: brown and extra-refined. Every year, the factory's sales executives study how customers' preferences change between the two types of sugar. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Brown** | **Extra-Refined** |\n| To: | **Brown** | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n| **Extra-Refined** | $0.2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $20%$ of customers who currently buy brown sugar will switch to extra-refined sugar next year, while $100%$ of customers who currently purchase extra-refined sugar will switch to brown sugar next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $450\\,000$ customers in total and that the given p